"# Where does each Spark component run?\n\nIn Apache Spark, different components run on different machines or processes depending on the Cluster Manager and deployment mode (Client or Cluster).\n\nThe Driver runs either on the submitting machine or inside the cluster, while Executors run on worker nodes.\n\n## 1. Component-wise location\n\n| Component       | Where does it run?                                                                                |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Driver          | On the client/submitting machine in Client Mode, or inside the cluster in Cluster Mode            |\n| SparkSession    | Inside the Driver process                                                                         |\n| SparkContext    | Inside the Driver process                                                                         |\n| DAG Scheduler   | Inside the Driver process                                                                         |\n| Task Scheduler  | Inside the Driver process                                                                         |\n| Cluster Manager | Runs as cluster management services, depending on YARN, Kubernetes, or Standalone                 |\n| Worker Node     | A physical or virtual machine in the cluster                                                      |\n| Executor        | Runs as a process on a worker node                                                                |\n| Tasks           | Execute inside Executors, generally using threads and CPU cores                                   |\n| Cached Data     | Primarily stored in Executor memory, with disk storage depending on the persistence configuration |\n\n## 2. Driver location — Client Mode vs Cluster Mode\n\nThis is the most important distinction.\n\n\n### Client Mode\n\n```\nspark-submit \\\n  --master yarn \\\n  --deploy-mode client \\\n  job.py\n```\n\n- Driver runs on the machine initiating the Spark application.\n- Executors run on cluster worker nodes.\n- The client must remain available while the application runs.\n\n### Cluster Mode\n\n```\nspark-submit \\\n  --master yarn \\\n  --deploy-mode cluster \\\n  job.py\n```\n\n- Driver runs inside the cluster.\n- Executors run on cluster worker nodes.\n- The submitting machine does not need to remain connected after successful submission.\n\n## 3. Where do components run with different Cluster Managers?\n\n| Deployment                    | Driver location                         | Executor location                             | Resource management                    |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| YARN Client Mode              | Submitting client machine               | YARN containers on worker nodes               | YARN ResourceManager and NodeManagers  |\n| YARN Cluster Mode             | Inside YARN ApplicationMaster container | YARN containers on worker nodes               | YARN ResourceManager and NodeManagers  |\n| Kubernetes Cluster Mode       | Driver Pod on a Kubernetes worker node  | Executor Pods on Kubernetes worker nodes      | Kubernetes control plane and scheduler |\n| Spark Standalone Client Mode  | Submitting machine                      | Spark worker nodes                            | Standalone Master and Worker daemons   |\n| Spark Standalone Cluster Mode | Launched on a Spark worker node         | Spark worker nodes                            | Standalone Master and Worker daemons   |\n| Local Mode                    | Local machine                           | Local JVM, without separate cluster Executors | Local Spark execution environment      |\n\n## 4. Interview-ready answer\n\n> In Apache Spark, the location of components depends on the deployment mode and cluster manager.\n>\n> The Driver runs on the submitting machine in Client Mode, whereas in Cluster Mode it runs inside the cluster. SparkSession, SparkContext, DAG Scheduler, and Task Scheduler are part of the Driver.\n>\n> Executors run on worker nodes and execute tasks using allocated CPU cores and memory. Tasks run inside Executor processes rather than on separate machines.\n>\n> The Cluster Manager runs as cluster management services. For example, YARN uses a ResourceManager and NodeManagers, while Kubernetes uses its control plane to manage resources and schedule pods.\n>\n> In production, Cluster Mode is commonly preferred for scheduled Spark applications because the Driver runs inside the cluster and does not depend on the submitting client remaining available.\n\nKey distinction: The Driver does not necessarily run on the master node, and Executors do not each require a dedicated machine. Multiple Executors can run on the same worker node if resources permit."
